insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0521$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Gas Laws$q$, $q$Charles's Law and absolute zero$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Charles's Law and absolute zero.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "84csd7", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "There's no physical law that fixes zero volume at -100°C - this doesn't correspond to any recognized reference point in gas behavior, so it reads as a guess rather than a derived value.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This looks like a mix-up with 373 K, the boiling point of water in Kelvin - a different, unrelated reference temperature that has nothing to do with where gas volume theoretically vanishes.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "0°C is just the freezing point of water - a familiar temperature, but not the one where a gas's volume theoretically drops to zero. Don't let familiarity substitute for the actual physics here.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$According to Charles's law, the volume of a gas becomes zero at$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "-100°C", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "-273°C", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "-373°C", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "0°C", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Charles's law tells us that at constant pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its temperature in Kelvin. If you extrapolate that straight-line relationship back to where volume would theoretically hit zero, you land on absolute zero - which is -273°C (0 K). Real gases actually liquefy before reaching this point, but the theoretical value the law points to is -273°C, and that's exactly why this temperature is called 'absolute zero'.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: There's no physical law that fixes zero volume at -100°C - this doesn't correspond to any recognized reference point in gas behavior, so it reads as a guess rather than a derived value. C: This looks like a mix-up with 373 K, the boiling point of water in Kelvin - a different, unrelated reference temperature that has nothing to do with where gas volume theoretically vanishes. D: 0°C is just the freezing point of water - a familiar temperature, but not the one where a gas's volume theoretically drops to zero. Don't let familiarity substitute for the actual physics here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0522$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Syngas$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Reaction of carbon with steam (water gas).$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kt2ylw", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Carbon(IV) oxide would be the product if carbon burned completely in plenty of oxygen - but this reaction has no free oxygen gas involved at all, just carbon reducing steam, so the product is the incomplete-oxidation gas, CO.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Trioxocarbonate(IV) acid (H2CO3) forms when CO2 dissolves in water - that's a completely different, lower-temperature process, not what happens when steam meets red-hot carbon.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "No free oxygen gas is released in this reaction - the oxygen from steam ends up combined in the carbon monoxide product, it doesn't come out on its own.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When steam is passed over red-hot carbon, the substances produced are$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "hydrogen and carbon(II) oxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "hydrogen and carbon(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "hydrogen and trioxocarbonate(IV) acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "hydrogen, oxygen and carbon(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is the classic 'water gas' reaction: C(s) + H2O(g) → CO(g) + H2(g). Red-hot carbon isn't burning in excess oxygen here - it's reducing steam, pulling one oxygen atom off each water molecule to form carbon monoxide (carbon(II) oxide), while releasing hydrogen gas. The 'red-hot, limited-oxygen' setup is your cue that this is a reduction reaction producing CO, not full combustion producing CO2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Carbon(IV) oxide would be the product if carbon burned completely in plenty of oxygen - but this reaction has no free oxygen gas involved at all, just carbon reducing steam, so the product is the incomplete-oxidation gas, CO. C: Trioxocarbonate(IV) acid (H2CO3) forms when CO2 dissolves in water - that's a completely different, lower-temperature process, not what happens when steam meets red-hot carbon. D: No free oxygen gas is released in this reaction - the oxygen from steam ends up combined in the carbon monoxide product, it doesn't come out on its own.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0523$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Uses of aluminium compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Uses of aluminium compounds.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4yozg9", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "It's colourless and contributes no colour of its own - confusing the fixing agent with the actual colouring substance is a common slip.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "A dispersant would spread particles apart; aluminium hydroxide does the opposite job - it binds the dye firmly to the fabric rather than dispersing it.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Chemically it's a hydroxide, not a salt, and even if the classification were closer, 'salt' doesn't describe its functional role in the dyeing process at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Aluminium hydroxide is used in the dyeing industry as a$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "dye", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "dispersant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "salt", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "mordant", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Aluminium hydroxide itself is colourless - it doesn't dye anything on its own. Its job is to act as a mordant: it forms a bridge between the fabric fibres and the dye molecule, fixing the colour in place so it doesn't wash out. Whenever you see a compound described as helping a dye 'stick' rather than providing colour itself, think mordant.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: It's colourless and contributes no colour of its own - confusing the fixing agent with the actual colouring substance is a common slip. B: A dispersant would spread particles apart; aluminium hydroxide does the opposite job - it binds the dye firmly to the fabric rather than dispersing it. C: Chemically it's a hydroxide, not a salt, and even if the classification were closer, 'salt' doesn't describe its functional role in the dyeing process at all.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0524$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Transition Metals and d-Orbitals$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Variable oxidation states.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "aixz7h", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Every element has electrons in s orbitals - this isn't a special feature of transition metals and doesn't explain why their oxidation states vary while, say, sodium's doesn't.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Transition metals are defined by their d-block position, not the p orbitals - p orbitals are the domain of main-group elements, so this mixes up which sublevel is actually responsible.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Same mix-up as option C - it's the d orbitals, not the p orbitals, that give transition metals their flexible bonding behaviour.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Transition metals possess variable oxidation states because they have$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "electrons in the s orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "electrons in the d orbitals", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "partially filled p orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "a variable number of electrons in the p orbitals", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$The defining feature of transition metals is their partially filled d orbitals, which sit very close in energy to the outer s orbital. Because the energy gap is so small, different numbers of d electrons can be lost or involved in bonding depending on the reaction, which is exactly why elements like iron or manganese show several different oxidation states instead of just one.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Every element has electrons in s orbitals - this isn't a special feature of transition metals and doesn't explain why their oxidation states vary while, say, sodium's doesn't. C: Transition metals are defined by their d-block position, not the p orbitals - p orbitals are the domain of main-group elements, so this mixes up which sublevel is actually responsible. D: Same mix-up as option C - it's the d orbitals, not the p orbitals, that give transition metals their flexible bonding behaviour.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0525$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Allotropy$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Allotropes of carbon and their uses.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "1e6r0v", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Soot is impure carbon from incomplete combustion, mainly used as a pigment source - it doesn't have the porous structure needed to adsorb colour from a solution.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Lampblack is used as a filler and pigment in things like ink and rubber - useful for its own reasons, but not for adsorption-based decolourization.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Graphite is prized for lubrication and conducting electricity because of its layered structure - knowing 'carbon has different forms' without knowing which one adsorbs leads students here.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The allotrope of carbon used in the decolourization of sugar is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "soot", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "lampblack", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "graphite", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "charcoal", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Charcoal (specifically activated charcoal) has a huge internal surface area full of tiny pores, which lets it adsorb coloured impurity molecules out of a sugar solution as it passes through. None of the other carbon forms have this porous, adsorbing structure - that's the specific property this question is testing.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Soot is impure carbon from incomplete combustion, mainly used as a pigment source - it doesn't have the porous structure needed to adsorb colour from a solution. B: Lampblack is used as a filler and pigment in things like ink and rubber - useful for its own reasons, but not for adsorption-based decolourization. C: Graphite is prized for lubrication and conducting electricity because of its layered structure - knowing 'carbon has different forms' without knowing which one adsorbs leads students here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0526$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Hybridization$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Carbon hybridization and tetravalency.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "lbnthq", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Only the outer 2s and 2p orbitals hybridize - the inner 1s core electrons take no part in bonding at all, so 'all' the orbitals is an overreach.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "It's not that every electron in every orbital is equivalent - specifically the valence 2s and 2p orbitals combine to create four new equivalent orbitals; the core electrons are untouched.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is close but skips the actual mechanism - the 2s and 2p electrons don't just happen to be 'equivalent', they become equivalent specifically because the orbitals hybridize. Leaving out that word misses what the question is really testing.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Carbon is tetravalent because$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals hybridized", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "all the atomic orbitals of carbon hybridize", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "the electrons in all the orbitals of carbon are equivalent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "the electrons in both the 2s and 2p orbitals are equivalent", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Carbon's ground state is 1s2 2s2 2p2, which on its own would only give it two unpaired electrons. To form four bonds, one 2s electron is promoted into the empty 2p orbital, and then the 2s and three 2p orbitals mix together - hybridize - to form four identical sp3 orbitals. That hybridization step is exactly why carbon ends up tetravalent with four equivalent bonds, not just 'having' four electrons somewhere.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Only the outer 2s and 2p orbitals hybridize - the inner 1s core electrons take no part in bonding at all, so 'all' the orbitals is an overreach. C: It's not that every electron in every orbital is equivalent - specifically the valence 2s and 2p orbitals combine to create four new equivalent orbitals; the core electrons are untouched. D: This is close but skips the actual mechanism - the 2s and 2p electrons don't just happen to be 'equivalent', they become equivalent specifically because the orbitals hybridize. Leaving out that word misses what the question is really testing.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0527$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$General Reactivity Facts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Storage and reactivity of sodium.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vfmsto", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Sodium doesn't undergo any meaningful reaction with atmospheric nitrogen under normal conditions - this option invents a mechanism that isn't the real threat.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "It's true sodium reacts violently with water - but that's exactly why it's never stored under water. This option confuses the reaction that makes water dangerous with the actual reason oil is chosen instead.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Oxygen is indeed a threat, but pairing it with carbon(IV) oxide overspecifies the danger - the standard concern is reaction with air's oxygen and moisture, not CO2 specifically.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Sodium metal is always kept under oil because it$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "is reduced by atmospheric nitrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "readily reacts with water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "reacts with oxygen and carbon(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "reacts vigorously on exposure to air", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Sodium is so reactive that ordinary air - its oxygen and moisture - will attack it almost immediately, tarnishing the shiny surface and eating into the metal. Oil is chosen as the storage medium precisely because it's unreactive and forms a barrier that excludes air completely. Don't rush to 'sodium reacts with water' here - that fact is actually why you'd never store it under water; oil is the safe, inert alternative that simply keeps air away.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Sodium doesn't undergo any meaningful reaction with atmospheric nitrogen under normal conditions - this option invents a mechanism that isn't the real threat. B: It's true sodium reacts violently with water - but that's exactly why it's never stored under water. This option confuses the reaction that makes water dangerous with the actual reason oil is chosen instead. C: Oxygen is indeed a threat, but pairing it with carbon(IV) oxide overspecifies the danger - the standard concern is reaction with air's oxygen and moisture, not CO2 specifically.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0528$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Alloys$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Preparation of alloys.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4ihrpp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Reducing metallic oxides is how you extract pure metals from their ores in the first place - it's a step before alloying, not the alloying process itself.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Arc-welding joins separate pieces of metal together at a seam - it doesn't create a uniform mixture of metals throughout a material the way alloying does.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Electroplating deposits a thin layer of one metal onto the surface of another using electrolysis - that's a coating process, completely different from forming a bulk homogeneous alloy.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Alloys are best prepared by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "cooling a molten mixture of the metals", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "reducing a mixture of their metallic oxides", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "arc-welding", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "electroplating", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$An alloy is a homogeneous blend of metals at the atomic level, and the simplest way to achieve that is to melt the metals together so they mix thoroughly as liquids, then let the mixture cool and solidify into a uniform solid. That melt-and-cool process is what actually creates the intermingled structure that defines an alloy.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Reducing metallic oxides is how you extract pure metals from their ores in the first place - it's a step before alloying, not the alloying process itself. C: Arc-welding joins separate pieces of metal together at a seam - it doesn't create a uniform mixture of metals throughout a material the way alloying does. D: Electroplating deposits a thin layer of one metal onto the surface of another using electrolysis - that's a coating process, completely different from forming a bulk homogeneous alloy.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0529$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Sulphur Compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Bleaching action of sulphur(IV) oxide.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "2eamtt", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Bleaching isn't about adding water to the coloured molecule - hydration is an unrelated process and doesn't explain how the colour is chemically destroyed.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This confuses SO2's chemical bleaching with the physical adsorption process used by charcoal - SO2 actually changes the chemical structure of the dye through a redox reaction, it doesn't just trap it on a surface.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the mechanism for chlorine's bleaching action, not sulphur(IV) oxide's - mixing up the two most commonly compared bleaching agents is a classic trap.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Sulphur(IV) oxide bleaches by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "hydration", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "reduction", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "absorption", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "oxidation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$This is a favourite JAMB contrast question: chlorine bleaches by oxidation, but sulphur(IV) oxide bleaches by the opposite mechanism - reduction. SO2 donates electrons to the coloured compound, chemically altering it into a colourless form. Whenever you see 'SO2 bleaching', pair it with 'reduction' in your head, and pair 'chlorine bleaching' with 'oxidation' - that contrast is exactly what examiners like to test.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Bleaching isn't about adding water to the coloured molecule - hydration is an unrelated process and doesn't explain how the colour is chemically destroyed. C: This confuses SO2's chemical bleaching with the physical adsorption process used by charcoal - SO2 actually changes the chemical structure of the dye through a redox reaction, it doesn't just trap it on a surface. D: This is the mechanism for chlorine's bleaching action, not sulphur(IV) oxide's - mixing up the two most commonly compared bleaching agents is a classic trap.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0530$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Gas Collection Methods$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Methods of gas collection based on density.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ewcz01", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Oxygen's molar mass (32) is only slightly above air's, so in practice it's usually collected over water rather than singled out for downward delivery based on density.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Hydrogen is far less dense than air, so it rises rather than sinks - it needs the opposite method (upward delivery, using an inverted jar), not downward delivery.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Like hydrogen, ammonia is lighter than air and rises, so it also needs upward delivery - lumping all 'special case' gases into one collection method without checking each one's density is what leads here.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following gases can be collected by the method of downward delivery?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Hydrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chlorine", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Downward delivery means the gas is piped down into an upright jar and, because it's denser than air, sinks to the bottom and pushes the lighter air out over the top. Chlorine (molar mass 71) is much denser than air (average molar mass about 29), so it fits this method perfectly. The trick to these questions is always to compare the gas's molar mass with air's - denser gases go by downward delivery, lighter ones need the inverted-jar 'upward delivery' method instead.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Oxygen's molar mass (32) is only slightly above air's, so in practice it's usually collected over water rather than singled out for downward delivery based on density. B: Hydrogen is far less dense than air, so it rises rather than sinks - it needs the opposite method (upward delivery, using an inverted jar), not downward delivery. D: Like hydrogen, ammonia is lighter than air and rises, so it also needs upward delivery - lumping all 'special case' gases into one collection method without checking each one's density is what leads here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0531$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$General Reactivity Facts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Calcium oxide and carbon reduction reactions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "12lk1z", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Trioxocarbonate(IV) (carbonate) forms when CaO reacts with carbon dioxide gas, not when it's heated directly with solid carbon - this mixes up two different reactions.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Calcium is far too reactive to be reduced to the free metal by carbon under these furnace conditions - that's why industry uses electrolysis to extract calcium metal, not carbon reduction.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "The gas released in this reaction is carbon monoxide (CO), not carbon(IV) oxide (CO2) - a common slip when assuming all carbon-oxide by-products default to CO2.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When calcium oxide is heated with carbon, the oxide will$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "be converted to carbide", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "be converted to the trioxocarbonate(IV) salt", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "decompose to the metal", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "melt, producing carbon(IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is the industrial reaction for making calcium carbide: CaO + 3C → CaC2 + CO, carried out at very high temperature in an electric furnace. Carbon doesn't just reduce CaO to the free metal here - calcium is too reactive for carbon to pull it all the way to the elemental state under these conditions. Instead, carbon combines directly with the calcium to form the carbide, releasing carbon monoxide as the by-product.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Trioxocarbonate(IV) (carbonate) forms when CaO reacts with carbon dioxide gas, not when it's heated directly with solid carbon - this mixes up two different reactions. C: Calcium is far too reactive to be reduced to the free metal by carbon under these furnace conditions - that's why industry uses electrolysis to extract calcium metal, not carbon reduction. D: The gas released in this reaction is carbon monoxide (CO), not carbon(IV) oxide (CO2) - a common slip when assuming all carbon-oxide by-products default to CO2.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0532$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Qualitative Analysis of Ions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Test for trioxocarbonate(IV) (carbonate) ions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kv2dlm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Nitrates don't typically release a gas on simple treatment with dilute HCl under these conditions, so there's no gas here to turn limewater milky.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "A chloride already contains the Cl- ion, so adding HCl produces no new reaction or gas at all - there's nothing for it to evolve.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "A hydroxide reacting with HCl is a straightforward neutralization producing salt and water - no gas is released, so it can't be the source of the limewater test.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A solution which, on treatment with hydrochloric acid, produces a colourless, odourless gas that turns limewater milky is most likely to be a$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "trioxonitrate(V)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "trioxocarbonate(IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "hydroxide", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$'Turns limewater milky' is the signature confirmatory test for carbon(IV) oxide gas - nothing else on this list produces that reaction. Carbonates (trioxocarbonate(IV) salts) react with HCl to release CO2: CO3^2- + 2H+ → H2O + CO2. Whenever you see 'milky limewater' in a question, your mind should jump straight to CO2, and from there straight to a carbonate.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Nitrates don't typically release a gas on simple treatment with dilute HCl under these conditions, so there's no gas here to turn limewater milky. B: A chloride already contains the Cl- ion, so adding HCl produces no new reaction or gas at all - there's nothing for it to evolve. D: A hydroxide reacting with HCl is a straightforward neutralization producing salt and water - no gas is released, so it can't be the source of the limewater test.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0533$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Alloys$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Composition and uses of soft solder.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4ihrpp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Iron has a very high melting point and isn't part of any soft solder formulation - it would defeat the whole purpose of a low-melting joining alloy.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Lead is correctly part of the picture, but its solder partner is tin, not zinc - zinc shows up in other alloys like brass and galvanizing, not soft solder.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This pairing actually describes galvanized iron (zinc-coated iron for corrosion protection), not an alloy used for soldering joints.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The soft solder used for welding and plumbing is an alloy of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "lead and tin", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "iron and tin", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "lead and zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "iron and zinc", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Soft solder is a lead-tin alloy, valued precisely because mixing the two metals gives a much lower melting point than either metal alone - exactly what you need for joining pipes and wires without damaging them with excessive heat.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Iron has a very high melting point and isn't part of any soft solder formulation - it would defeat the whole purpose of a low-melting joining alloy. C: Lead is correctly part of the picture, but its solder partner is tin, not zinc - zinc shows up in other alloys like brass and galvanizing, not soft solder. D: This pairing actually describes galvanized iron (zinc-coated iron for corrosion protection), not an alloy used for soldering joints.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0534$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Iron Compounds and Redox Behaviour$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Chemical composition of rust.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gc697d", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Plain Fe2O3 (without water of hydration) is a dry, anhydrous compound - real rust always incorporates water from the atmosphere, so leaving out the 'xH2O' misses what actually forms on exposed iron.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "FeO is iron(II) oxide, where iron is in the +2 state - but rust forms through oxidation to the +3 state, so this is the wrong oxidation level entirely.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Fe3O4 (magnetite) is a mixed iron(II,III) oxide with a black colour, found in different contexts like the natural mineral or the black scale from hot iron - it's not the reddish-brown compound formed by everyday atmospheric rusting.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The reddish-brown rust on iron roofing sheets consists of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Fe2O3 (anhydrous)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fe2O3.xH2O (hydrated iron(III) oxide)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "FeO (iron(II) oxide)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fe3O4 (iron(II,III) oxide)", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Rust isn't a single dry oxide - it forms when iron reacts with both oxygen and moisture in the air, so it comes out as hydrated iron(III) oxide, Fe2O3.xH2O, where x is a variable amount of water trapped in the structure. That water content is exactly why rust looks flaky and porous rather than like a solid metallic oxide coating.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Plain Fe2O3 (without water of hydration) is a dry, anhydrous compound - real rust always incorporates water from the atmosphere, so leaving out the 'xH2O' misses what actually forms on exposed iron. C: FeO is iron(II) oxide, where iron is in the +2 state - but rust forms through oxidation to the +3 state, so this is the wrong oxidation level entirely. D: Fe3O4 (magnetite) is a mixed iron(II,III) oxide with a black colour, found in different contexts like the natural mineral or the black scale from hot iron - it's not the reddish-brown compound formed by everyday atmospheric rusting.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0535$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Rates of Reaction$q$, $q$Rate of Reaction — Temperature and Collision Theory$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Factors affecting reaction rate.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ocems4", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Raising temperature does speed up the reaction, but dropping the concentration works against it at the same time - the two effects partly cancel out instead of combining for the greatest increase.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Pressure and volume are the relevant rate factors for reactions involving gases, not for a reaction happening entirely between species dissolved in aqueous solution - this applies the wrong variable to the wrong type of reaction.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Lowering the temperature actually slows the reaction down, even while raising concentration speeds it up - again, the two changes pull in opposite directions rather than maximizing rate together.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Na2S2O3(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + H2O(l) + ... . Which of the following would introduce the greatest increase in the rate of the chemical reaction above?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "An increase in temperature and a decrease in the concentration of the reactant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "A decrease in the volume and an increase in the pressure of the reactant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "A decrease in the temperature and an increase in the concentration of the reactant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "An increase in temperature and an increase in the concentration of the reactant", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Rate of reaction responds to both temperature and concentration in the same direction - raising either one speeds things up, because you get more particles moving faster (more frequent, more energetic collisions) and more particles packed into the same space (more frequent collisions overall). To get the greatest possible increase, you want both factors pushing the rate up at once, which only happens in option D. Pressure and volume changes matter for gas-phase reactions, but this is an aqueous solution reaction, so they're irrelevant here.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Raising temperature does speed up the reaction, but dropping the concentration works against it at the same time - the two effects partly cancel out instead of combining for the greatest increase. B: Pressure and volume are the relevant rate factors for reactions involving gases, not for a reaction happening entirely between species dissolved in aqueous solution - this applies the wrong variable to the wrong type of reaction. C: Lowering the temperature actually slows the reaction down, even while raising concentration speeds it up - again, the two changes pull in opposite directions rather than maximizing rate together.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0536$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Halogens — Starch-Iodide Test$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Uses and preparation of iodine tincture.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "izl9kl", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Bromine chloride isn't the solvent used for iodine tincture at all - it's an unrelated interhalogen compound with no role here.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Chlorine water would actually react with dissolved iodide rather than serve as a solvent for molecular iodine - it's not how tincture of iodine is prepared.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Iodine barely dissolves in plain water - leaving out the ethanol misses the whole reason tincture of iodine needs an alcohol-based solvent in the first place.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The tincture of iodine means iodine dissolved in$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "ethanol and water", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "bromine chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "chlorine water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "water", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Tincture of iodine is the familiar antiseptic solution made by dissolving iodine in a mixture of ethanol and water (with a trace of potassium iodide to help it dissolve). Iodine is only very sparingly soluble in water on its own, which is exactly why the alcohol is needed to get it into solution in the first place.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Bromine chloride isn't the solvent used for iodine tincture at all - it's an unrelated interhalogen compound with no role here. C: Chlorine water would actually react with dissolved iodide rather than serve as a solvent for molecular iodine - it's not how tincture of iodine is prepared. D: Iodine barely dissolves in plain water - leaving out the ethanol misses the whole reason tincture of iodine needs an alcohol-based solvent in the first place.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0537$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Coordinate Bonding — Ligands$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Le Chatelier's principle and complex ion equilibria.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "25qyuy", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "NH3 isn't consumed here - it's actually released back into solution as the complex ion dissociates to replace the Cu2+ being removed by precipitation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is the opposite direction from what Le Chatelier's principle predicts - removing a species from the left side pulls the equilibrium left, not right, to replace what's lost.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Removing one of the equilibrium species is exactly the kind of disturbance Le Chatelier's principle addresses - assuming 'no effect' skips over the core idea the question is testing.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Cu2+(aq) + 4NH3(aq) ↔ [Cu(NH3)4]2+(aq). In the reaction above, what is the effect of precipitating Cu2+(aq) as CuS(s)?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "NH3 is completely consumed", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "More NH3 is released as the equilibrium shifts to the left", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "The equilibrium will shift to the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "There will be no effect", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Cu2+ sits on the left side of this equilibrium, as one of the reactants forming the deep-blue complex ion. If you precipitate Cu2+ out of solution as insoluble CuS, you're constantly removing it from the left side. By Le Chatelier's principle, the system responds by shifting to replace what's being removed - that means the equilibrium shifts left, and the complex ion [Cu(NH3)4]2+ breaks back down to regenerate Cu2+ (which then gets precipitated again), releasing free NH3 into the solution as a result.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: NH3 isn't consumed here - it's actually released back into solution as the complex ion dissociates to replace the Cu2+ being removed by precipitation. C: This is the opposite direction from what Le Chatelier's principle predicts - removing a species from the left side pulls the equilibrium left, not right, to replace what's lost. D: Removing one of the equilibrium species is exactly the kind of disturbance Le Chatelier's principle addresses - assuming 'no effect' skips over the core idea the question is testing.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0538$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Reactivity and Corrosion Resistance$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Resistance to oxidation and metal uses.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "5q74k0", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "'Easily reduced' describes how readily a metal ion gains electrons, which isn't the relevant property here - the question is about resisting oxidation (corrosion) in everyday use, not about reduction.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This swaps in the wrong process - durability against corrosion is about resisting oxidation, not about how easily the metal can be reduced.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is the exact opposite of the correct answer - a metal that oxidizes easily would corrode and tarnish quickly, making it a poor choice for coins meant to last.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Copper and silver metals are commonly used as coins because they are$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "easily reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "not easily reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "easily oxidized", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "not easily oxidized", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$A good coin metal needs to keep its shine and resist corrosion through years of handling. Copper and silver sit low in the reactivity series, meaning they don't readily lose electrons to oxygen or moisture in the air - in other words, they're not easily oxidized. That resistance to tarnishing and corroding is exactly why they've historically been chosen for coinage.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 'Easily reduced' describes how readily a metal ion gains electrons, which isn't the relevant property here - the question is about resisting oxidation (corrosion) in everyday use, not about reduction. B: This swaps in the wrong process - durability against corrosion is about resisting oxidation, not about how easily the metal can be reduced. C: This is the exact opposite of the correct answer - a metal that oxidizes easily would corrode and tarnish quickly, making it a poor choice for coins meant to last.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0539$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Reactivity and Electrochemical Series$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Reaction of metals with dilute acids.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6wz62", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Iron and tin would react fine, but copper is mixed in here - and copper sits below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it won't dissolve in dilute HCl, breaking the set.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Magnesium and iron are reactive enough, but copper again spoils the set - assuming any 'common metal' reacts with acid without checking its actual reactivity series position is the trap here.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Magnesium and tin would dissolve just fine, but copper is included once more - the same reactivity series gap trips up this option too.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following sets of metals dissolves in dilute HCl?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Fe, Sn and Cu", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mg, Fe and Cu", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mg, Sn and Fe", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cu, Mg and Sn", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$The rule here is simple: only metals positioned above hydrogen in the reactivity series can displace it from dilute acid and dissolve. Magnesium, tin, and iron all sit above hydrogen, so all three react with dilute HCl to release hydrogen gas and dissolve as chloride salts. Copper sits below hydrogen - it simply can't do this. The trick with this question is to check every single metal in each set, because one 'bad' metal (copper) is enough to disqualify the whole option.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Iron and tin would react fine, but copper is mixed in here - and copper sits below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it won't dissolve in dilute HCl, breaking the set. B: Magnesium and iron are reactive enough, but copper again spoils the set - assuming any 'common metal' reacts with acid without checking its actual reactivity series position is the trap here. D: Magnesium and tin would dissolve just fine, but copper is included once more - the same reactivity series gap trips up this option too.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0540$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Chlorine Water and Photodecomposition$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Photodecomposition of chlorine water.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gbh920", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "By the time sunlight acts on the solution, the original chlorine gas has already reacted to form HCl and HOCl - what decomposes further is the HOCl, not leftover free chlorine gas.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This adds in free hydrogen gas, which doesn't appear anywhere in this photodecomposition - only oxygen is released, while the hydrogen stays combined as HCl in solution.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This gets the direction backwards - hypochlorous acid is the species that's already present and breaking down to release oxygen, not something newly formed by the sunlight reaction.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$If chlorine is bubbled into water and the resulting yellowish-green solution is exposed to bright sunlight for a while, the solution will decompose, giving out$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "oxygen and leaving behind aqueous hydrochloric acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "chlorine and oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "oxygen, hydrogen and chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "oxygen, thereby producing hypochlorous acid", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Don't rush past what's already happened by the time you get to the 'sunlight' step. When chlorine first dissolves in water, it reacts to form a mixture of hydrochloric acid and hypochlorous acid: Cl2 + H2O ↔ HCl + HOCl. It's this hypochlorous acid that's unstable in sunlight and breaks down further: 2HOCl → 2HCl + O2. So the gas given off is oxygen, and what's left behind in solution is aqueous hydrochloric acid - the hypochlorous acid has already been consumed in the process, not produced by it.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: By the time sunlight acts on the solution, the original chlorine gas has already reacted to form HCl and HOCl - what decomposes further is the HOCl, not leftover free chlorine gas. C: This adds in free hydrogen gas, which doesn't appear anywhere in this photodecomposition - only oxygen is released, while the hydrogen stays combined as HCl in solution. D: This gets the direction backwards - hypochlorous acid is the species that's already present and breaking down to release oxygen, not something newly formed by the sunlight reaction.$q$);
